%S A402238 1,1,1,4,55,1511,68926,4724917,455173909,58716223309,9780121203256,
%T A402238 2044711278327851,524530179082664899,162067563577631252191,
%U A402238 59386370617793959112920,25471393957213050337295911,12644896353794208290229714121,7195233591897658003360034881433
%N A402238 Number of essential acyclic digraphs (or DAGs) on n labeled nodes in which every node has in-degree at most 2.
%C A402238 An acyclic digraph is essential if it is the only member of its Markov equivalence class, that is, if no other acyclic digraph has the same skeleton and the same v-structures (a -> c <- b with a and b not adjacent); see Andersson, Madigan and Perlman.
%C A402238 Equivalently, by a theorem of Chickering, it has no covered arc: an arc u -> v is covered if the parents of v are u together with the parents of u.
%C A402238 Equivalently, a(n) is the number of Bayesian network structures on n variables, each variable with at most 2 parents, that are the only acyclic digraph on these variables with their conditional independence relations.
%C A402238 Without the bound on the in-degree the count is A026586. With in-degree at most 1 it is 1 for every n, since an arc out of a root of a rooted forest is covered.
%C A402238 a(n) = A026586(n) for n <= 3; a(4) = 55 against A026586(4) = 59.
%D A402238 R. W. Robinson, Counting labeled acyclic digraphs, in F. Harary, ed., New Directions in the Theory of Graphs, Academic Press, NY, 1973, pp. 239-273.
%H A402238 Tyler Satchel Orden, <a href="/A402238/b402238.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..60</a>
%H A402238 Steen A. Andersson, David Madigan and Michael D. Perlman, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1031833662">A characterization of Markov equivalence classes for acyclic digraphs</a>, Ann. Statist., 25 (1997), 505-541.
%H A402238 Davide Bizzaro, Luciano Serafini and Sagar Malhotra, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.13736">Towards Counting Markov Equivalence Classes with Logical Constraints</a>, arXiv:2405.13736 [cs.LO], 2024. See Table 1.
%H A402238 David Maxwell Chickering, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.4938">A Transformational Characterization of Equivalent Bayesian Network Structures</a>, arXiv:1302.4938 [cs.AI], 2013; Proc. 11th Conf. on Uncertainty in Artificial Intelligence (UAI 1995), 87-98.
%H A402238 Bertran Steinsky, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0012-365X(02)00838-5">Enumeration of labelled chain graphs and labelled essential directed acyclic graphs</a>, Discrete Math., 270 (2003), 267-278.
%F A402238 Let E(n,i,j) be the number of these digraphs with exactly i nodes of in-degree 0 and exactly j nodes of in-degree 1. Then E(0,0,0) = 1 and, for n >= 1, E(n,i,j) = Sum_{t=1..n} (-1)^(t+1)*binomial(n,t) * Sum_{s0+s1+s2=t} t!/(s0!*s1!*s2!) * (m-i+s0)^s1 * (binomial(m,2)-j+s1)^s2 * E(m,i-s0,j-s1), where m = n-t, and a(n) = Sum_{i,j} E(n,i,j).
%F A402238 (Inclusion-exclusion over sets of t sinks, as in Robinson's recurrence for A003024 and Steinsky's recurrence for A026586. A sink added to an essential digraph D on m nodes creates no covered arc if and only if its set of parents is not of the form {u} union parents(u); these m sets are distinct, and the number of them of size r+1 is the number of nodes of D with in-degree r.)
%e A402238 a(3) = 4: the digraph with no arcs and the three digraphs a -> c <- b.
%e A402238 a(4) = 55: of the 59 essential acyclic digraphs on 4 nodes, 4 consist of one node with the other three as its parents.
%o A402238 (Python)
%o A402238 from math import comb
%o A402238 def aList(N):  # a(0), ..., a(N)
%o A402238     P = [{(0, 0): 1}]  # P[m][(i,j)]: i nodes of in-degree 0, j nodes of in-degree 1
%o A402238     for n in range(1, N+1):
%o A402238         new = {}
%o A402238         for t in range(1, n+1):
%o A402238             m = n-t
%o A402238             for (i, j), w in P[m].items():
%o A402238                 for s1 in range(t+1):
%o A402238                     for s2 in range(t-s1+1):
%o A402238                         x = (-1)**(t+1)*comb(n, t)*comb(t, s1)*comb(t-s1, s2)*(m-i)**s1*(comb(m, 2)-j)**s2*w
%o A402238                         if x:
%o A402238                             key = (i+t-s1-s2, j+s1)
%o A402238                             new[key] = new.get(key, 0) + x
%o A402238         P.append(new)
%o A402238     return [sum(p.values()) for p in P]
%o A402238 print(aList(17))
%Y A402238 Cf. A026586 (no bound on the in-degree), A007984 (Markov equivalence classes).
%Y A402238 Cf. A397711 (all acyclic digraphs with in-degree at most 2).
%K A402238 nonn,new
%O A402238 0,4
